UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 34, Lesson 1: Types of angles', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Angles are turns between two rays; classify as right (square corner), acute (smaller) or obtuse (larger than right but less than straight).","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nClassify: a sharp pizza slice tip; a square tile corner; a wide road intersection corner that is more than square.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAcute; right; obtuse.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCalling every “pointy” shape acute without comparing to a right angle misclassifies.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nClassify twenty angles as acute, right or obtuse. Sketch six examples and mark a right-angle square symbol where relevant.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Angles are turns between two rays; classify as right (square corner), acute (smaller) or obtuse (larger than right but less than straight).","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-2DS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Classify twenty angles as acute, right or obtuse. Sketch six examples and mark a right-angle square symbol where relevant. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for angle; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Calling every “pointy” shape acute without comparing to a right angle misclassifies.. Correction uses Acute; right; obtuse.. Why: restores accurate angle.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Calling every “pointy” shape acute without comparing to a right angle misclassifies. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses angle with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with angle, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for angle, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about angle, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for angle. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling every “pointy” shape acute without comparing to a right angle misclassifies","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Acute; right; obtuse"],"question":"Classify: a sharp pizza slice tip; a square tile corner; a wide road intersection corner that is more than square. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for angle; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of right and ex